@@@@ open-branch-prediction
situation: open
topic: Branch prediction
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["guess"],"askMustNot":["mispredict","unsorted (run|array|one) (is|takes|will)"]}
noverify: an opening; the cycle figure is hedged as typical and the question's answer is not stated
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A pipelined processor fetches the instructions after a conditional branch several cycles before it knows which way the branch goes, so it guesses, and a wrong guess throws away the work done since. On current desktop and server cores that is typically somewhere around 15 to 20 cycles each time. Branch prediction is why branch-free comparisons appear in database engines, parsers and sorting code, and why compilers turn some `if` statements into conditional moves. It is also where speculative execution starts, and with it the Spectre class of attacks disclosed in 2018.

<ask type="predict">A loop adds up the elements of a large array that are at least 128; the elements are random bytes from 0 to 255. You run it on the array as it is, then on a sorted copy. Both runs perform the same additions. Which run do you expect to take longer, and what inside the processor would cause the difference?</ask>

